  "name": "Example: \n                    Identifying an Additive Inverse",
  "problem": "How can we represent the additive inverse of $-2$ on the number line?",
  "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of a number is the number that lies at the same distance from zero on the number line, but on the opposite side. \n\nAs we can see on the number line below, the distance of $-2$ from zero is $2$ units. The only other number that is at a distance of $2$ units from zero is $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14152428/assets/035ad7581b3084ab33917ceb4560b46054871f96551b30825d5c14239a7c0687.png)\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of $-2$ is $2$, and it can be represented on the number line as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14152428/assets/75b5ed5f417e89d2ed58f5b2892f43bdbf64ca44da2e7b031a18a9147fd2940a.png)",
